  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-VNM126</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved quality of education system providing gender-responsive, equitable and climate resilient, equipping all with relevant, transferrable, digital and life skills, even during emergencies</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Viet Nam, a high percentage of workers, especially those in manufacturing and processing sectors, are still unskilled or low-skilled, which hampers the sectoral upgrade into the higher-added value chains and places the country with potential skills shortages for highly skilled occupations. In addition, the Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) system also needs to improve its quality and relevance to match the labour market demand.
Over the years, the ILO has provided assistance to the tripartite constituents in Viet Nam to identify opportunities for anticipating and developing new skills to address future demands of the textile and garment industry. Based on a Skill for Trade and Economic Diversification (STED) study  ILO supports the development of a sectoral skills strategy in collaboration with the Viet Nam Textile and Apparel Association (VITAS) to address future skills needs and also to contribute to the effective implementation of the Skills Strategy for Viet Nam&apos;s Textile and Garment Industry.</narrative>
  </description>
